# authorized_user_phones.conf -- who check_authorized_user_phone_wifi.sh looks for.
#
# Copy this file to authorized_user_phones.conf next to the script (in Home
# Assistant's config directory, /config) and fill it in. The real file is NOT
# committed: a WiFi MAC identifies a device.
#
# One line per AUTHORIZED PRIMARY-ROLE user of THIS location, i.e. the people
# who actually live here / use it (ontology `location_role: primary`).
# Do NOT list a maintenance-only user (someone who set the location up and
# maintains it but is not an occupant): their phone must not make the location
# read as occupied; they are a guest for presence purposes.
#
# Columns, whitespace separated:
#   person_id   the id used in the presence topic, e.g. nate -> cabin/presence/nate
#   wifi_mac    the MAC the phone presents ON THIS NETWORK (see the note below)
#   lan_cidr    the LAN to sweep so the ARP cache is warm, e.g. 192.168.2.0/24
#
# Note: Android and iOS may present a private (randomized) MAC per network. The
# MAC listed here must be the one the router actually sees, or the check can
# never match. Find it in the router's client list, or `ip neigh` on the hub
# while the phone is connected. See homeassistant/PRESENCE.md.
#
# person_id  wifi_mac           lan_cidr
# alex       aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff  192.168.2.0/24
